-- title:
--   Points dictionary of the p-divisible group into J₁(Mp)
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a natural number $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of conductor $p$, with $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity, and let $K$ be the intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ given by [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), that is, the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, with $p$ in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $\zeta$ in the image of $A$ in $L$, and with $K$ an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. Let $j \in K$ be the element whose Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), with $j \ne 0$. All the geometry takes place over the two-chart model [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, obtained by gluing the spectra of the subalgebras of $K$ integral over $A[j]$ and over $A[j^{-1}]$, and assumed proper over $\operatorname{Spec} A$.
--
--   The hypotheses fall into the following groups, each of which is described here in summary.
--
--   *Special fibre.* A field $k$, algebraically closed of characteristic $p$ and an $A$-algebra; two schemes $C_1, C_2$ with proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral structure morphisms $c_1, c_2$ to $\operatorname{Spec} k$; closed immersions $i_1, i_2$ of $C_1, C_2$, over $c_1, c_2$, into the base change of the model to $k$; the hypothesis `hcover`, that every point of that base change lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred`, that the fibre product of $i_1$ and $i_2$ is reduced; and a natural number $n$ with `hn` asserting $\operatorname{card}$ of that fibre product equal to $n$ and `hn0` asserting $0 < n$.
--
--   *Sections.* A section $\varepsilon$ of the model over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, sections $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$ of $c_1, c_2$, and `hε₁`, which says that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $k$.
--
--   *Relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ data.* A designation $D$ over $A$ for the model (a scheme $D.P$ with structure morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a zero section), together with `hrep`: the relative sub-Picard functor cut out by the condition `algEquivZeroCut` (fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero of rigidified line bundles over all algebraically closed field points) is represented by $D$, with a rigidified Poincaré bundle whose pullbacks classify such bundles uniquely up to isomorphism and which is trivial along the zero section; `hsm` and `hsep`, that $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ is smooth and separated; `hreps`, the corresponding representability statement after base change to $k$ by the base-changed designation; `hPk`, an isomorphism of the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` with the one obtained from that of `hrep` by base change along $\operatorname{Spec} k$; designations $D_1, D_2$ for $c_1, c_2$ with representability statements `hrep₁`, `hrep₂`; a morphism $\nu_2$ from the $k$-base change of $D$ to $D_2$ over the base morphisms; and `hν₂`, which states that for every $k$-scheme $t$ and every $t$-point $a$ of the $k$-base change of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep₂` along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification, along `rigSection c₂ t ε₂` and the second projection, of the pullback along `curveChange i₂` of the bundle classified by $a$.
--
--   *Generic geometric model.* Compatible algebra structures of $A$ and $L$ on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; `hsmL` and `hgiL`, smoothness of relative dimension $1$ and geometric integrality of the $L$-base change of the model; `hprL` and `hgcL`, properness and geometric connectedness of the $L$-base change of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$; a curve model $M_\eta$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for the base-changed function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) (an integral scheme, proper and smooth of relative dimension $1$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, with an isomorphism of its function field with that field and a bijection between its closed points and the places); an isomorphism $e_\eta$ from $M_\eta.C$ to the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-fibre of the model with `heη` expressing compatibility of structure morphisms; nonemptiness of the preimage of the finite chart under $e_\eta$ followed by the first projection; `hMηpin`, which pins down, for every element $a$ of the finite chart algebra, the Laurent expansion over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the corresponding function-field element of $M_\eta$ as the coefficientwise image of $a$ under $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; and `hgal`, the Galois reading law: for every automorphism $g$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ fixing $L$ pointwise and all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M_\eta.C$ with $x'$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec}(g)$ followed by $x$ in the finite chart, the place attached to $x'$ is the image of the place attached to $x$ under [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54) of $g$.
--
--   *Hecke inputs and semilinear action.* The predicates [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L58) and [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondCommuteBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L54), the latter giving the commutation of the Hecke and diamond generators and hence the `HeckeAlgOne`-module structure on $J_1(Mp)$; a multiplicative semiring action of $\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ on $A$ with `hΓA` asserting that it is compatible with $A \to L$.
--
--   *Special fibre groups.* A structure $G$ consisting of abelian groups $J^0_s$, $J^I$, $J^E$, a subgroup $\mathrm{torus}$ of $J^0_s$ and a surjection $\mathrm{proj} : J^0_s \to J^I \times J^E$ with kernel $\mathrm{torus}$; bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` of $J^0_s$, $J^I$, $J^E$ with the $k$-points of the base of the $k$-base change of $D$, of $D_1$ and of $D_2$; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE`, which say that these bijections turn addition into tensor product of the corresponding pullbacks of the relevant Poincaré bundles; and `hproj`, which computes the two components of $\mathrm{proj}$ as composition with the classifying morphism attached to $i_1$ and with $\nu_2$.
--
--   *Generic dictionary, Hecke and Galois operators.* A bijection `gpts` of $J_1(Mp) = \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the base-changed function field with the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$; a map $\varphi$ from `HeckeAlgOne` to endomorphisms of $D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$; a map $\tau$ assigning to each $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ a morphism of $D.P$ over the $s$-twist of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$; and the compatibilities `hφmul` ($\varphi(t)$ respects the relative group law of `hrep`), `hφpts` (`gpts` intertwines the Hecke action with composition by $\varphi(t)$), `hτ1`, `hτmul` ($\tau$ is multiplicative in diagrammatic order), `hτφ` ($\tau(s)$ and $\varphi(t)$ commute), `hgadd` (`gpts` is additive for the relative group law) and `hτpts` (for $\sigma'$ in $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ restricting to $s$ on $L$, `gpts`$(\sigma' \cdot x)$ is $\operatorname{Spec}(\sigma')$ followed by `gpts`$(x)$ followed by $\tau(s^{-1})$).
--
--   *Abel–Jacobi data.* Representability `hDL` after base change to $L$; a morphism $\mathrm{aj}_L$ from the $L$-fibre of the model to the base of the $L$-base change of $D$; a morphism $k_L$ from the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-fibre to the $L$-fibre of the model; a morphism $\overline{\mathrm{aj}} : M_\eta.C \to D.P$; a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $\overline{\varepsilon}$ of $M_\eta.C$; `hPL` (base change of the Poincaré bundle to $L$); `hajLε` ($\mathrm{aj}_L$ sends the base section to the zero section); `hajL`, the defining formula, that for every field $K'$ and every $K'$-point $x$ of the $L$-fibre the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hDL` along $x$ followed by $\mathrm{aj}_L$ is the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the base point; `hkL₁`, `hkL₂` (compatibility of $k_L$ with the two projections, the second up to $\operatorname{Spec} \overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to \operatorname{Spec} L$); `hajbar` ($\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ is $e_\eta$ followed by $k_L$, $\mathrm{aj}_L$ and the first projection); `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj` (compatibilities of $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$ and $\overline{\varepsilon}$ with the structure morphisms, with $\varepsilon$ and with the zero section); and `hpts_aj`, which says that for $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x$ and $s$ of $M_\eta.C$ with $s$ the base point there is a degree-zero divisor equal to the difference of the places of $x$ and $s$ whose class is carried by `gpts` to $x$ followed by $\overline{\mathrm{aj}}$.
--
--   *The abelian subscheme.* A scheme $\mathcal{A}$ with structure morphism $a$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a closed immersion $\iota$ of $\mathcal{A}$ into $D.P$ over $a$; `h𝒜pr` and `h𝒜sm`, that $a$ is proper and smooth; `h𝒜conn`, that all geometric fibres of $a$ are connected; `h𝒜grp`, that the points of $\mathcal{A}$ contain a unit and are closed under the multiplication and inversion of the relative group law of `hrep` after composition with $\iota$; `h𝒜gen`, that an element $x$ of $J_1(Mp)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) — the image of the endomorphism $|S|\cdot \mathrm{id} - \sum_{d \in S} \langle d\rangle$, where $S$ is the set of $d < Mp$ coprime to $Mp$ with $d \equiv 1 \pmod{M}$ — if and only if `gpts`$(x)$ factors through $\iota$ by a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $a$; and `h𝒜hecke`, that the points of $\mathcal{A}$ are stable under composition with $\varphi(t)$.
--
--   *Valuation data.* A valuation subring $P\ell$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ in which $p$ is a non-unit, a ring map $\rho : A \to P\ell$ with `hρ` recovering $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and $\pi_k : P\ell \to k$ with `hAlgk` recovering $A \to k$ and `hπk` saying that the kernel of $\pi_k$ is the maximal ideal of $P\ell$.
--
--   *Group law on $\mathcal{A}$ and the $p$-divisible group.* A relative group law $L_{\mathcal{A}}$ for $a$ over $A$, commutative by `hL𝒜c`, with `hL𝒜ι` asserting that $\iota$ carries it to the relative group law of `hrep`; a natural number $h$ and a $p$-divisible group $G_A$ over $A$ of height data $(p,h)$, that is, a tower of finite free cocommutative Hopf $A$-algebras $G_A.\mathrm{level}\,v$ of rank $p^{vh}$ with surjective transition maps whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals; an additive map $\iota_{G,\mathrm{pts}}$ from $G_A.\mathrm{Points}\,\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (the direct limit of the additive groups of the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of the levels, an $A$-algebra map being multiplied by convolution) to the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $a$ with the group structure from $L_{\mathcal{A}}$; morphisms $\iota_{G}(v) : \operatorname{Spec}(G_A.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to \mathcal{A}$; and the clauses `hPinj` (injectivity of $\iota_{G,\mathrm{pts}}$), `hPgal` (equivariance for $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/A)$), `hPtors` (every $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $\mathcal{A}$ killed by some power of $p$ lies in the range of $\iota_{G,\mathrm{pts}}$), `hS1` ($\iota_G(v)$ lies over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the structural map $A \to G_A.\mathrm{level}\,v$), `hS2` (each $\iota_G(v)$ is a closed immersion), `hS3` ($\iota_G(v)$ followed by multiplication by $p^v$ is the unit section), `hS4` (the image under $\iota_{G,\mathrm{pts}}$ of a level-$v$ point $x$ is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the $A$-algebra map of $x$ followed by $\iota_G(v)$) and `hS5` (for every $A$-algebra $B$, $\iota_G(v)$ carries the convolution product of two $B$-points of level $v$ to the $L_{\mathcal{A}}$-product).
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exists an additive map
--   $$\Delta : G_A.\mathrm{Points}\,\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \longrightarrow J_1(Mp)$$
--   into $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) with the following four properties.
--
--   First, $\Delta$ is injective.
--
--   Second, for every $v \in \mathbb{N}$ and every $y \in J_1(Mp)$: $p^v \cdot y = 0$ and $y$ lies in [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) if and only if $y = \Delta$ of the image in the direct limit of some $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $x$ of $G_A.\mathrm{level}\,v$. Thus the image of the level-$v$ points is exactly the $p^v$-torsion of the norm-free part.
--
--   Third, for every $\tau \in \operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ and every $\tau'$ an algebra automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over $A$ such that $\tau'$ and $\tau$ agree as functions on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and every $z \in G_A.\mathrm{Points}\,\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, one has $\Delta(\tau' \cdot z) = \tau \cdot \Delta(z)$.
--
--   Fourth, the reading law: for every $v$ and every $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $x$ of $G_A.\mathrm{level}\,v$, the morphism underlying `gpts` of $\Delta$ of the image of $x$ in the direct limit equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the ring map underlying the $A$-algebra map of $x$, followed by $\iota_G(v)$, followed by $\iota$.
--
--   This is the points dictionary between the $p$-divisible group of the norm-free abelian subscheme of the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the two-chart model of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}[\zeta_p]$ and the norm-free part of $J_1(Mp)(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$, the map $\Delta$ being pinned uniquely by the requirement that it be read through the level immersions inside $D$. It is used in the analysis of the Tate module of the norm-free part, where the valuations of the coordinates of its points at a place above $p$ are compared with the reduction of the corresponding sections.
